{"chapter": "topologies", "node_kind": "section_title", "parent_tag": "0ETA", "placeholders": {}, "render": {"prefix": "\\section{", "suffix": "}\n\\label{section-V}"}, "risk_level": "R1", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:98ee3ad3fbe8fb7c823b1a15c63aea07bfb12bdf41833b70c474b922a4ce6eb5", "source_text": "The V topology", "source_text_hash": "sha256:d2e5b66a369080d39889c5fe28b19476d900a5a650895ca023230d9f392c5782", "unit_id": "tag:0ETA:title"}
{"chapter": "topologies", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "0ETA", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$v$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{lemma-refine-qcqs-V}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:7d3aea4cb5529003becd6e36794568a152d9ec6a0b9b0ac6257ea4cc67cecf49", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:7ad291c66afc028f34b5051ff28b4cf61d629dcd76bd56a5f94f3e912cdf405b", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nThe V topology is stronger than all other topologies in this chapter.\nRoughly speaking it is generated by Zariski coverings and\nby quasi-compact morphisms satisfying a lifting property\nfor specializations (Lemma <REF_0001>).\nHowever, the procedure we will use to define V coverings is a bit different.\nWe will first define standard V coverings of affines and then\nuse these to define V coverings in general. Typographical point:\nin the literature sometimes ``<MATH_0001>-covering'' is used instead of ``V covering''.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:2b6314c47a3d83c6aa04f08845b78dbf905cf51f084a1254fe81ec2d58682508", "unit_id": "tag:0ETA:prose-0001"}
{"chapter": "topologies", "node_kind": "definition", "parent_tag": "0ETA", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$T$", "MATH_0002": "$\\{T_j \\to T\\}_{j = 1, \\ldots, m}$", "MATH_0003": "$T_j$", "MATH_0004": "$g : \\Spec(V) \\to T$", "MATH_0005": "$V$", "MATH_0006": "$V \\subset W$", "MATH_0007": "$1 \\leq j \\leq m$", "MATH_0008": "$$\n\\xymatrix{\n\\Spec(W) \\ar[r] \\ar[d] & T_j \\ar[d] \\\\\n\\Spec(V) \\ar[r]^g & T\n}\n$$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{more-algebra-definition-extension-valuation-rings}", "TEXTITCLOSE_0001": "}", "TEXTITOPEN_0001": "{\\it "}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{definition}\n\\label{definition-standard-V-covering}\n", "suffix": "\\end{definition}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:dff190b69dd179c291284128530fc6340eccdfc640c343b1b5b556779ab0936e", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:18d2573fbe9936a7ffcdcb487136b114b80f4042be362e2f9da3e0b0f86c6e8f", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> be an affine scheme. A <TEXTITOPEN_0001>standard V covering<TEXTITCLOSE_0001> is a finite family\n<MATH_0002> with <MATH_0003> affine\nsuch that for every morphism <MATH_0004> where <MATH_0005>\nis a valuation ring, there is an extension <MATH_0006> of valuation rings\n(More on Algebra, Definition\n<REF_0001>),\nan index <MATH_0007>, and a commutative diagram\n<MATH_0008>\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:3e3f2654cd651bd39e3618de2fa16fae330bc38ef972adedec0782e78ec3dc9b", "unit_id": "tag:0ETB:statement"}
{"chapter": "topologies", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "0ETA", "placeholders": {"STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:e5ea1c6df99c12ccc2e66bffff376b90f8c7f4f24be23fe6b6975692a1568dfa", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nWe first prove a few basic lemmas about this notion.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:fbb6753bd940bc15e77993ce5931476f12b9d66e182221b367558c7e0cabd1b5", "unit_id": "tag:0ETA:prose-0002"}
{"chapter": "topologies", "node_kind": "lemma", "parent_tag": "0ETA", "placeholders": {}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{lemma}\n\\label{lemma-standard-fpqc-standard-V}\n", "suffix": "\\end{lemma}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:739aaf754a2a1a29a120543c8fbb0a0a9e2bd4c0c1f3a341af74044a59d04ae5", "source_text": "A standard fpqc covering is a standard V covering.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:4a37a3209af5bbfa875a4282a6a7c7f3e013de050d4474b79775936aa08ec642", "unit_id": "tag:0ETC:statement"}
{"chapter": "topologies", "node_kind": "proof", "parent_tag": "0ETA", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\{X_i \\to X\\}_{i = 1, \\ldots, n}$", "MATH_0002": "$g : \\Spec(V) \\to X$", "MATH_0003": "$V$", "MATH_0004": "$x \\in X$", "MATH_0005": "$\\Spec(V)$", "MATH_0006": "$i$", "MATH_0007": "$x_i \\in X_i$", "MATH_0008": "$x$", "MATH_0009": "$\\Spec(V) \\times_X X_i$", "MATH_0010": "$x'_i$", "MATH_0011": "$\\Spec(V)$", "MATH_0012": "$\\Spec(V) \\times_X X_i \\to \\Spec(V)$", "MATH_0013": "$x''_i \\leadsto x'_i$", "MATH_0014": "$\\Spec(V) \\times_X X_i$", "MATH_0015": "$x''_i$", "MATH_0016": "$\\Spec(V)$", "MATH_0017": "$W$", "MATH_0018": "$h : \\Spec(W) \\to \\Spec(V) \\times_X X_i$", "MATH_0019": "$h$", "MATH_0020": "$\\Spec(W)$", "MATH_0021": "$x''_i$", "MATH_0022": "$\\Spec(W)$", "MATH_0023": "$x'_i$", "MATH_0024": "$$\n\\xymatrix{\n\\Spec(W) \\ar[r] \\ar[d] & X_i \\ar[d] \\\\\n\\Spec(V) \\ar[r] & X\n}\n$$", "MATH_0025": "$V \\to W$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{definition-standard-fpqc}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{morphisms-lemma-generalizations-lift-flat}", "REF_0003": "\\ref{schemes-lemma-points-specialize}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{proof}\n", "suffix": "\\end{proof}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:3731854bf492e8ea27dc49e6c6755c3ffc50c3c392c9ecb295a8013daf8561ca", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:da99b9044c53337829a47326622d3719d494d935f4723f9f78d1df0633008727", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> be a standard fpqc covering\n(Definition <REF_0001>). Let <MATH_0002>\nbe a morphism where <MATH_0003> is a valuation ring. Let <MATH_0004> be the\nimage of the closed point of <MATH_0005>. Choose an <MATH_0006> and a point\n<MATH_0007> mapping to <MATH_0008>. Then <MATH_0009>\nhas a point <MATH_0010> mapping to the closed point of <MATH_0011>.\nSince <MATH_0012> is flat\nwe can find a specialization <MATH_0013> of\npoints of <MATH_0014> with <MATH_0015> mapping to\nthe generic point of <MATH_0016>, see\nMorphisms, Lemma <REF_0002>. By\nSchemes, Lemma <REF_0003>\nwe can choose a valuation ring <MATH_0017> and a morphism\n<MATH_0018> such that <MATH_0019>\nmaps the generic point of <MATH_0020> to <MATH_0021> and the\nclosed point of <MATH_0022> to <MATH_0023>. We obtain a\ncommutative diagram\n<MATH_0024>\nwhere <MATH_0025> is an extension of valuation rings.\nThis proves the lemma.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:dc649eef227ab137a3b52bd63b58c16f46786249114b32b69fae5e9481664bf4", "unit_id": "tag:0ETC:proof"}
